  "id": "DrugBank_DB11110 - MONDO_2319",
  "verbalization": "Magnesium Citrate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Phosphorus Metabolism Disease through a series of indirect pathways involving hypercalcemia. The drug is linked to Hypercalcemia Disease, which in turn is associated with several phosphorus-related compounds, such as Calcium Acetate, Calcium Glucoheptonate, and Calcium Glycerophosphate. These compounds are all involved in the regulation of phosphate homeostasis, suggesting a potential role for magnesium citrate in modulating calcium levels, which may influence phosphate metabolism. The consistent presence of hypercalcemia as a mediator across different pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which magnesium citrate could affect phosphorus metabolism, possibly through calcium homeostasis. The evidence supports a hypothesis that magnesium citrate may influence phosphate metabolism via its effects on calcium levels, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
